[THOUGHT] The patient cannot recall whether a palpable gallbladder was noted — so that clinical sign remains unconfirmed. Given the repeated lack of return on imaging (MRCP, CT) and lab (CA 19-9) requests, and with 12/16 turns used, I must now select the *single highest-yield remaining available test* that is both clinically accessible and capable of directly characterizing the subhepatic cystic mass and biliary anatomy.  

Among available tests (per prior Analyst replies), **endoscopic ultrasound (EUS)** is highly likely to be available in most diagnostic gastroenterology or hepatobiliary settings — and it surpasses transabdominal ultrasound in resolution for periampullary and distal CBD lesions. EUS can:  
- Delineate whether the cystic mass is the CBD, gallbladder, pancreatic cyst, or a solid tumor with cystic degeneration;  
- Assess wall thickness, septations, nodules, and vascularity;  
- Detect subtle pancreatic head masses not seen on transabdominal US;  
- Enable fine-needle aspiration (FNA) if a suspicious lesion is found — providing cytologic diagnosis.  

It is more sensitive than CT or MRCP for small ampullary or distal CBD tumors and is the best next step when non-invasive imaging is unavailable or inconclusive.

[TEST] Endoscopic ultrasound (EUS)

